1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:01,734
Maryland.

2
00:00:01,734 --> 00:00:03,283
September 17, 1862.

3
00:00:03,283 --> 00:00:07,524
Along a creek called Antietam, two armies fight from

4
00:00:07,524 --> 00:00:08,828
dawn until dark.

5
00:00:09,278 --> 00:00:12,397
When it is over, about 23,000 men are killed,

6
00:00:12,397 --> 00:00:13,713
wounded or missing.

7
00:00:13,713 --> 00:00:17,040
The National Park Service calls it the bloodiest

8
00:00:17,040 --> 00:00:19,812
single day in American military history.

9
00:00:20,262 --> 00:00:24,141
The Union army's medical director counted 8,350 of

10
00:00:24,141 --> 00:00:25,382
his own wounded.

11
00:00:25,382 --> 00:00:29,804
And he wrote that many slight wounds were never recorded.

12
00:00:30,254 --> 00:00:31,936
So imagine you are one of them.

13
00:00:31,936 --> 00:00:33,021
A Union infantryman.

14
00:00:33,021 --> 00:00:34,812
You are nineteen, or thirty-five.

15
00:00:34,812 --> 00:00:36,548
You have been marching for days.

16
00:00:36,998 --> 00:00:39,710
The bullet that hits you is soft lead, about an ounce.

17
00:00:39,710 --> 00:00:41,166
It does not make a neat hole.

18
00:00:41,616 --> 00:00:43,048
There are no antibiotics.

19
00:00:43,048 --> 00:00:44,823
Surgeons know nothing of germs.

20
00:00:44,823 --> 00:00:47,628
And the place where you will be cut open may be a

21
00:00:47,628 --> 00:00:50,319
barn full of hay, still inside the range of the

22
00:00:50,319 --> 00:00:51,064
enemy's guns.

23
00:00:51,514 --> 00:00:53,581
But something is about to change.

24
00:00:53,581 --> 00:00:56,463
A 37-year-old army doctor has spent the summer

25
00:00:56,463 --> 00:00:59,720
building a system to get men like you off the field.

26
00:00:59,720 --> 00:01:01,913
Antietam is one of its first tests.

27
00:01:02,363 --> 00:01:05,026
This is what it was like to be wounded in the

28
00:01:05,026 --> 00:01:06,150
American Civil War.

29
00:01:06,150 --> 00:01:09,110
From the moment the bullet hit, to the saw, to the

30
00:01:09,110 --> 00:01:11,003
hospital, to the long road home.

31
00:01:11,003 --> 00:01:13,903
From the Army's own records, and the words of the

32
00:01:13,903 --> 00:01:14,909
men who lived it.

33
00:01:15,359 --> 00:01:16,210
Wounded.

34
00:01:16,210 --> 00:01:17,698
The Civil War.

35
00:01:20,098 --> 00:01:21,289
It started with a bullet.

36
00:01:22,889 --> 00:01:27,523
Almost every wound a Union surgeon treated came from

37
00:01:27,523 --> 00:01:28,058
a gun.

38
00:01:28,058 --> 00:01:32,603
The Army's official medical history counted 245,790

39
00:01:32,603 --> 00:01:33,940
gunshot wounds.

40
00:01:34,390 --> 00:01:37,664
Sabres and bayonets, the weapons of old paintings,

41
00:01:37,664 --> 00:01:39,562
caused fewer than one in 200.

42
00:01:40,012 --> 00:01:42,625
Most of those bullets were the Minié ball:

43
00:01:42,625 --> 00:01:45,859
a cone of soft lead with a hollow base that expanded

44
00:01:45,859 --> 00:01:48,534
into the rifling, so it spun and flew true.

45
00:01:48,984 --> 00:01:51,837
One modern estimate, in the magazine Civil War

46
00:01:51,837 --> 00:01:54,814
Monitor, credits the Minié ball with about three

47
00:01:54,814 --> 00:01:57,729
quarters of all the war's battlefield injuries.

48
00:01:58,179 --> 00:01:59,515
Accurate, and heavy.

49
00:01:59,515 --> 00:02:02,319
When soft lead struck a man, it flattened.

50
00:02:02,319 --> 00:02:05,790
In the words of the Army's medical museum, shattered

51
00:02:05,790 --> 00:02:09,195
bone and shredded flesh became the calling cards of

52
00:02:09,195 --> 00:02:10,197
the Minié ball.

53
00:02:10,647 --> 00:02:13,859
The official history noted that bullets came out of

54
00:02:13,859 --> 00:02:16,441
bodies deformed in every imaginable form.

55
00:02:16,891 --> 00:02:19,726
Where you were hit decided almost everything.

56
00:02:19,726 --> 00:02:23,002
Of the wounds Union surgeons treated, about seven in

57
00:02:23,002 --> 00:02:24,766
ten were in an arm or a leg.

58
00:02:25,216 --> 00:02:27,088
That number hides something.

59
00:02:27,088 --> 00:02:30,229
It only counts men who reached a surgeon alive.

60
00:02:30,229 --> 00:02:33,104
Many men hit in the head or body never did.

61
00:02:33,554 --> 00:02:36,043
For those who did, the odds were stark.

62
00:02:36,043 --> 00:02:39,106
A bullet that went through the wall of the belly

63
00:02:39,106 --> 00:02:42,424
killed 87 of every 100 men the surgeons could track.

64
00:02:42,874 --> 00:02:45,318
Through the chest, about six in ten died.

65
00:02:45,318 --> 00:02:48,299
Gunshot injuries of the skull, about six in ten of

66
00:02:48,299 --> 00:02:50,028
those who reached a hospital.

67
00:02:50,477 --> 00:02:52,455
Arms and legs were different.

68
00:02:52,455 --> 00:02:53,478
You could live.

69
00:02:53,478 --> 00:02:56,820
But a soft lead ball that splintered a thigh bone

70
00:02:56,820 --> 00:02:59,889
left a surgeon in 1862 with very few choices.

71
00:03:00,339 --> 00:03:02,763
Think about what that meant on the day.

72
00:03:02,763 --> 00:03:05,995
A man hit in the arm could often walk to the rear on

73
00:03:05,995 --> 00:03:06,492
his own.

74
00:03:06,492 --> 00:03:08,419
A man hit in the leg could not.

75
00:03:08,419 --> 00:03:11,713
He lay where he fell, and waited for someone to come.

76
00:03:12,163 --> 00:03:15,319
A New York private wounded at Gettysburg the next

77
00:03:15,319 --> 00:03:18,925
summer described the first moments in a single sentence.

78
00:03:18,925 --> 00:03:22,209
After my leg was shattered, I fell down, laying for

79
00:03:22,209 --> 00:03:23,883
a few minutes unconscious.

80
00:03:24,333 --> 00:03:27,468
A private in the 135th Pennsylvania, shot by

81
00:03:27,468 --> 00:03:31,173
accident the day after the battle, wrote home from a

82
00:03:31,173 --> 00:03:32,883
hospital near the field.

83
00:03:33,333 --> 00:03:36,109
The ball passed through my lungs and lodged some

84
00:03:36,109 --> 00:03:37,496
where, and is in me yet.

85
00:03:38,296 --> 00:03:41,059
He died less than three weeks after he was hit.

86
00:03:41,059 --> 00:03:44,351
And for every wounded man, the next question was simple.

87
00:03:44,351 --> 00:03:45,879
Would anyone come for you?

88
00:03:46,329 --> 00:03:49,083
In the first year of the war, the answer was often no.

89
00:03:50,683 --> 00:03:54,187
At the first battle of Bull Run, in July 1861, the

90
00:03:54,187 --> 00:03:57,621
Army's ambulances were driven by hired civilians.

91
00:03:57,621 --> 00:04:00,074
Many of them abandoned their posts.

92
00:04:00,524 --> 00:04:03,688
The Army's own history records wounded men left for

93
00:04:03,688 --> 00:04:06,727
long periods unattended on the field, and walking

94
00:04:06,727 --> 00:04:10,077
wounded who made their own way back toward Washington.

95
00:04:10,527 --> 00:04:12,575
Nobody truly owned the ambulances.

96
00:04:12,575 --> 00:04:15,646
Medical officers and quartermasters shared control,

97
00:04:15,646 --> 00:04:18,657
and the man who later fixed the system wrote that,

98
00:04:18,657 --> 00:04:22,090
as a natural consequence, neither took much care of them.

99
00:04:22,540 --> 00:04:25,249
Worse, officers used them as private carriages.

100
00:04:25,249 --> 00:04:28,189
Letterman wrote that they were too often used as if

101
00:04:28,189 --> 00:04:31,186
they had been made for the convenience of commanding

102
00:04:31,186 --> 00:04:31,705
officers.

103
00:04:32,155 --> 00:04:35,243
And many of the early ambulances had just two wheels.

104
00:04:35,693 --> 00:04:39,295
In May 1861, the Army had ordered 200 of them built.

105
00:04:39,295 --> 00:04:41,788
On rough roads, they were a torment.

106
00:04:42,238 --> 00:04:45,902
The government's own medical history did not soften it.

107
00:04:45,902 --> 00:04:48,966
Their motion was intolerable and excruciating.

108
00:04:48,966 --> 00:04:51,298
Wounded men begged to be taken out.

109
00:04:51,748 --> 00:04:55,088
In the spring of 1862, the Union army moved up the

110
00:04:55,088 --> 00:04:57,427
Virginia Peninsula toward Richmond.

111
00:04:57,427 --> 00:05:00,767
Its medical director, Charles Tripler, called many

112
00:05:00,767 --> 00:05:03,239
of the two-wheelers good for nothing.

113
00:05:03,689 --> 00:05:07,472
On June 28, with too few ambulances, Tripler had to

114
00:05:07,472 --> 00:05:11,181
leave more than 600 wounded behind, to be taken by

115
00:05:11,181 --> 00:05:11,923
the enemy.

116
00:05:12,373 --> 00:05:15,019
Then came the end of August, and the second battle

117
00:05:15,019 --> 00:05:15,654
at Bull Run.

118
00:05:16,104 --> 00:05:19,072
This time, more than 8,000 casualties flooded in.

119
00:05:19,072 --> 00:05:22,160
The system, the Army's history says, was simply not

120
00:05:22,160 --> 00:05:23,251
prepared for them.

121
00:05:23,701 --> 00:05:27,267
In Washington, 250 ambulances were scraped together

122
00:05:27,267 --> 00:05:28,806
in a single afternoon.

123
00:05:28,806 --> 00:05:29,785
Most were new.

124
00:05:29,785 --> 00:05:32,303
The horses had never been harnessed.

125
00:05:32,753 --> 00:05:34,708
The drivers were hired in a hurry.

126
00:05:34,708 --> 00:05:37,354
A report later printed in the Official Records

127
00:05:37,354 --> 00:05:38,964
conceded what happened next.

128
00:05:39,414 --> 00:05:42,495
A few of the drivers got scared and ran away.

129
00:05:42,495 --> 00:05:43,590
A few got drunk.

130
00:05:44,040 --> 00:05:46,087
And the men who could not walk?

131
00:05:46,087 --> 00:05:49,522
In the words of the Army's official medical history,

132
00:05:49,522 --> 00:05:52,361
they lay by the thousands on the ground for

133
00:05:52,361 --> 00:05:54,937
twenty-four and more hours, unattended.

134
00:05:55,387 --> 00:05:56,082
Picture it.

135
00:05:56,082 --> 00:05:58,738
A late-summer field in Virginia. No shade.

136
00:05:58,738 --> 00:06:00,887
No water unless someone brings it.

137
00:06:00,887 --> 00:06:02,215
And you cannot stand.

138
00:06:02,665 --> 00:06:05,575
Ten days after the battle, one historian at

139
00:06:05,575 --> 00:06:08,960
Gettysburg National Military Park found, about 600

140
00:06:08,960 --> 00:06:12,073
wounded were still waiting to be carried away.

141
00:06:12,523 --> 00:06:15,555
A volunteer from Massachusetts went to that field

142
00:06:15,555 --> 00:06:17,041
with wagons of supplies.

143
00:06:17,041 --> 00:06:18,650
Her name was Clara Barton.

144
00:06:18,650 --> 00:06:20,073
We will meet her again.

145
00:06:20,523 --> 00:06:23,137
There was one bright spot in that disaster.

146
00:06:23,137 --> 00:06:25,933
A few ambulances from the Army of the Potomac,

147
00:06:25,933 --> 00:06:29,033
already run under a new set of rules, had been sent

148
00:06:29,033 --> 00:06:30,613
to help at Pope's battles.

149
00:06:31,063 --> 00:06:33,914
When the reports came back, the difference between

150
00:06:33,914 --> 00:06:36,765
those ambulances and the rest, their author wrote,

151
00:06:36,765 --> 00:06:39,102
was very striking in favor of the former.

152
00:06:39,552 --> 00:06:41,089
Something had to change.

153
00:06:41,089 --> 00:06:44,291
And one man in the Army of the Potomac had already

154
00:06:44,291 --> 00:06:44,804
started.

155
00:06:45,254 --> 00:06:46,977
His name was Jonathan Letterman.

156
00:06:48,577 --> 00:06:51,909
He was an Army surgeon from Pennsylvania who had

157
00:06:51,909 --> 00:06:53,992
spent years at frontier posts.

158
00:06:53,992 --> 00:06:57,601
In the summer of 1862, he was ordered to the Army of

159
00:06:57,601 --> 00:06:58,434
the Potomac.

160
00:06:58,434 --> 00:07:01,975
He took charge of its medical department on July 4.

161
00:07:02,425 --> 00:07:05,320
He had been chosen by a reforming new Surgeon

162
00:07:05,320 --> 00:07:08,537
General, William Hammond, who wanted a modern army

163
00:07:08,537 --> 00:07:09,566
medical service.

164
00:07:10,016 --> 00:07:13,015
Letterman found an army worn out by disease and by

165
00:07:13,015 --> 00:07:14,395
the Peninsula fighting.

166
00:07:14,395 --> 00:07:17,094
He began with the problem that had failed the

167
00:07:17,094 --> 00:07:18,534
wounded again and again:

168
00:07:18,534 --> 00:07:20,153
getting them off the field.

169
00:07:20,603 --> 00:07:24,272
On August 2, 1862, at Harrison's Landing in

170
00:07:24,272 --> 00:07:28,452
Virginia, the army issued Special Orders No. 147.

171
00:07:28,452 --> 00:07:31,012
It created an ambulance corps.

172
00:07:31,462 --> 00:07:33,912
It was built like a small army of its own.

173
00:07:33,912 --> 00:07:35,663
A captain for each army corps.

174
00:07:35,663 --> 00:07:38,172
A lieutenant for each division and brigade.

175
00:07:38,172 --> 00:07:39,864
A sergeant for each regiment.

176
00:07:40,314 --> 00:07:42,589
Each regiment got its own ambulances:

177
00:07:42,589 --> 00:07:45,601
one four-horse and two two-horse wagons, with two

178
00:07:45,601 --> 00:07:46,769
stretchers in each.

179
00:07:46,769 --> 00:07:49,228
Each ambulance had a driver and two men.

180
00:07:49,678 --> 00:07:52,846
The light two-horse wagons brought men in from the field.

181
00:07:52,846 --> 00:07:55,569
The heavier four-horse wagons carried men who had

182
00:07:55,569 --> 00:07:57,848
already been treated further to the rear.

183
00:07:57,848 --> 00:07:59,460
Each job had its own vehicle.

184
00:07:59,910 --> 00:08:03,592
These were soldiers, permanently detailed from the ranks.

185
00:08:03,592 --> 00:08:05,401
Not teamsters. Not bandsmen.

186
00:08:05,401 --> 00:08:07,016
Not frightened civilians.

187
00:08:07,466 --> 00:08:10,240
They wore a mark so everyone would know them:

188
00:08:10,240 --> 00:08:13,260
a green band two inches broad around the cap, and

189
00:08:13,260 --> 00:08:15,233
green half-chevrons on the arms.

190
00:08:15,683 --> 00:08:17,942
And one rule changed the battlefield.

191
00:08:17,942 --> 00:08:20,934
No person will be allowed to carry from the field

192
00:08:20,934 --> 00:08:23,315
any wounded or sick, except this corps.

193
00:08:23,765 --> 00:08:26,876
Before, healthy men had left the firing line to help

194
00:08:26,876 --> 00:08:30,346
a wounded friend to the rear, and often did not come back.

195
00:08:30,346 --> 00:08:33,398
Now the fighting men kept fighting, and trained men

196
00:08:33,398 --> 00:08:34,415
did the carrying.

197
00:08:34,865 --> 00:08:36,449
They drilled like infantry.

198
00:08:36,449 --> 00:08:38,855
Letterman's orders went down to the feet:

199
00:08:38,855 --> 00:08:41,907
the front man steps off with the left foot, the rear

200
00:08:41,907 --> 00:08:44,958
man with the right, so the stretcher would not sway.

201
00:08:45,408 --> 00:08:48,313
Water kegs were rinsed and filled every day, so an

202
00:08:48,313 --> 00:08:50,346
ambulance could move at any moment.

203
00:08:50,796 --> 00:08:54,081
Stretchers alone weighed 23 to 26 pounds, before a

204
00:08:54,081 --> 00:08:55,460
man was laid on them.

205
00:08:55,460 --> 00:08:58,548
Early in the war, that work had often fallen to

206
00:08:58,548 --> 00:08:59,599
young musicians.

207
00:08:59,599 --> 00:09:02,884
A surgeon called them lads physically incapable of

208
00:09:02,884 --> 00:09:04,198
such fatiguing duty.

209
00:09:04,648 --> 00:09:07,882
Surgeon General Hammond wanted Letterman's corps in

210
00:09:07,882 --> 00:09:08,960
every Union army.

211
00:09:08,960 --> 00:09:11,814
The Secretary of War, Edwin Stanton, said no.

212
00:09:11,814 --> 00:09:14,921
It would only add expense, he argued, without any

213
00:09:14,921 --> 00:09:16,506
corresponding advantages.

214
00:09:16,956 --> 00:09:18,780
He also fixed the supplies.

215
00:09:18,780 --> 00:09:22,088
Letterman complained that many officers seemed to

216
00:09:22,088 --> 00:09:25,532
consider medical supplies the least important cargo

217
00:09:25,532 --> 00:09:29,380
in an army, and dumped them first when wagons were short.

218
00:09:29,830 --> 00:09:33,005
From October, medicines would be issued by brigade,

219
00:09:33,005 --> 00:09:35,371
and handed to the regiments as needed.

220
00:09:35,371 --> 00:09:38,234
A regiment's chest of medicine could no longer

221
00:09:38,234 --> 00:09:39,853
simply vanish on the road.

222
00:09:40,303 --> 00:09:42,609
And the vehicles themselves improved.

223
00:09:42,609 --> 00:09:45,538
The best of the later war was the Rucker wagon:

224
00:09:45,538 --> 00:09:48,530
four wheels, springs, about 1,100 pounds, with a

225
00:09:48,530 --> 00:09:51,584
water cask under the driver's seat and stretchers

226
00:09:51,584 --> 00:09:53,018
that folded into seats.

227
00:09:53,468 --> 00:09:55,785
The Army's own history called it the most

228
00:09:55,785 --> 00:09:59,063
serviceable ambulance wagon of the latter part of the war.

229
00:09:59,513 --> 00:10:02,352
So for now, it would exist only in the Army of the

230
00:10:02,352 --> 00:10:02,807
Potomac.

231
00:10:02,807 --> 00:10:05,476
And it was barely five weeks old when Robert E.

232
00:10:05,476 --> 00:10:07,350
Lee's army crossed into Maryland.

233
00:10:07,800 --> 00:10:08,666
Antietam.

234
00:10:10,266 --> 00:10:12,696
The line of battle ran six to seven miles.

235
00:10:12,696 --> 00:10:15,531
Letterman wrote that the hospitals were therefore

236
00:10:15,531 --> 00:10:16,341
very numerous.

237
00:10:16,791 --> 00:10:18,930
Letterman was on the field that day.

238
00:10:18,930 --> 00:10:21,782
No one engaged on that hotly contested field, he

239
00:10:21,782 --> 00:10:24,633
wrote, can ever forget the intense interest with

240
00:10:24,633 --> 00:10:27,307
which he watched the progress of that battle.

241
00:10:27,757 --> 00:10:31,250
In and around one 24-acre cornfield, the Park

242
00:10:31,250 --> 00:10:34,898
Service estimates, about 8,200 men were killed,

243
00:10:34,898 --> 00:10:37,770
wounded or missing, from both armies.

244
00:10:37,770 --> 00:10:41,418
At a sunken farm road later called Bloody Lane,

245
00:10:41,418 --> 00:10:42,350
about 5,400.

246
00:10:42,800 --> 00:10:45,717
Every one of those men who was still alive needed to

247
00:10:45,717 --> 00:10:47,849
be found, carried, sorted and treated.

248
00:10:47,849 --> 00:10:50,261
By an ambulance corps barely six weeks old.

249
00:10:50,711 --> 00:10:52,821
His new system was not fully ready.

250
00:10:52,821 --> 00:10:56,317
Many ambulances had been left behind or lost on the march.

251
00:10:56,317 --> 00:10:59,692
Two hundred new ones had arrived just before the battle.

252
00:10:59,692 --> 00:11:01,862
In his words, they were unorganized.

253
00:11:02,312 --> 00:11:03,937
He chose barns over houses.

254
00:11:03,937 --> 00:11:06,886
Barns well provided with hay and straw, he wrote,

255
00:11:06,886 --> 00:11:08,271
were better ventilated.

256
00:11:08,271 --> 00:11:11,340
Where he could, he laid the wounded in the open air

257
00:11:11,340 --> 00:11:12,062
beside them.

258
00:11:12,512 --> 00:11:15,615
Even so, he admitted, the hospitals were not always

259
00:11:15,615 --> 00:11:17,866
beyond the reach of the enemy's guns.

260
00:11:18,316 --> 00:11:21,291
Near the center of the line stood the Pry house,

261
00:11:21,291 --> 00:11:23,336
General McClellan's headquarters.

262
00:11:23,336 --> 00:11:26,558
Its barn became a field hospital for Union soldiers,

263
00:11:26,558 --> 00:11:28,975
until they could be moved to Frederick.

264
00:11:29,425 --> 00:11:32,108
General Joseph Hooker, wounded in the foot, was

265
00:11:32,108 --> 00:11:33,535
treated at the Pry house.

266
00:11:33,535 --> 00:11:36,560
The Park Service notes the wound had filled his boot.

267
00:11:37,010 --> 00:11:40,020
At the Roulette farm, which lay in the path of the

268
00:11:40,020 --> 00:11:43,090
Union advance on the Sunken Road, the barn became a

269
00:11:43,090 --> 00:11:44,956
field hospital for the wounded.

270
00:11:45,406 --> 00:11:48,470
Into one of those field hospitals came Clara Barton,

271
00:11:48,470 --> 00:11:51,358
with a wagon of supplies she had brought herself.

272
00:11:51,808 --> 00:11:54,356
She knelt to give a wounded man a drink.

273
00:11:54,356 --> 00:11:56,076
She felt her sleeve quiver.

274
00:11:56,076 --> 00:11:59,134
A bullet had passed between her body and the arm

275
00:11:59,134 --> 00:12:01,236
that held him, and it killed him.

276
00:12:01,686 --> 00:12:04,726
Later she asked, dryly, whether a soldier ever does

277
00:12:04,726 --> 00:12:06,574
mend a bullet hole in his coat.

278
00:12:06,574 --> 00:12:09,912
A surgeon there called her the angel of the battlefield.

279
00:12:10,362 --> 00:12:12,973
The surgeons worked without stopping.

280
00:12:12,973 --> 00:12:16,007
Major William Child, surgeon of the 5th New

281
00:12:16,007 --> 00:12:18,054
Hampshire, wrote to his wife:

282
00:12:18,054 --> 00:12:21,370
day before yesterday I dressed the wounds of 64

283
00:12:21,370 --> 00:12:22,358
different men.

284
00:12:22,808 --> 00:12:24,523
And he wrote what he saw.

285
00:12:24,523 --> 00:12:27,954
The poor wounded, mutilated soldiers that yet have

286
00:12:27,954 --> 00:12:31,178
life and sensation, make a most horrid picture.

287
00:12:31,628 --> 00:12:34,864
A New York soldier remembered comrades with wounds

288
00:12:34,864 --> 00:12:37,971
of all conceivable shapes, brought in and placed

289
00:12:37,971 --> 00:12:38,812
side by side.

290
00:12:39,262 --> 00:12:41,129
Now the ambulance corps was tested.

291
00:12:41,129 --> 00:12:43,849
Letterman reported that the wounded on the right of

292
00:12:43,849 --> 00:12:46,569
the line were brought in before two o'clock the day

293
00:12:46,569 --> 00:12:47,689
following the battle.

294
00:12:48,139 --> 00:12:49,284
It was not perfect.

295
00:12:49,284 --> 00:12:51,996
Some men were overlooked, and lay out longer.

296
00:12:51,996 --> 00:12:54,890
The Army's own history says some remained on the

297
00:12:54,890 --> 00:12:58,024
battlefield as much as twenty-four hours, and at the

298
00:12:58,024 --> 00:13:00,134
time, critics attacked the service.

299
00:13:00,584 --> 00:13:03,088
But compared with Second Bull Run, three weeks

300
00:13:03,088 --> 00:13:04,830
earlier, it was a different war.

301
00:13:05,280 --> 00:13:08,468
Letterman's men also took in more than 2,000

302
00:13:08,468 --> 00:13:11,512
Confederate wounded left behind. He wrote:

303
00:13:11,512 --> 00:13:15,280
humanity teaches that a wounded and prostrate foe is

304
00:13:15,280 --> 00:13:16,657
no longer an enemy.

305
00:13:17,107 --> 00:13:20,697
At a farm hospital, one blinded Confederate soldier

306
00:13:20,697 --> 00:13:21,894
was heard to say:

307
00:13:21,894 --> 00:13:25,413
some darned Yank has shot away my eyes, but I feel

308
00:13:25,413 --> 00:13:27,384
as strong otherwise as ever.

309
00:13:27,834 --> 00:13:29,553
Then came the long road out.

310
00:13:29,553 --> 00:13:32,562
After the battle, Letterman set up two large tent

311
00:13:32,562 --> 00:13:34,097
hospitals near the field.

312
00:13:34,097 --> 00:13:37,228
He called them the first of their kind attempted in

313
00:13:37,228 --> 00:13:38,026
this country.

314
00:13:38,026 --> 00:13:40,789
The larger, at Smoketown, had about 600 beds.

315
00:13:41,239 --> 00:13:44,693
The worst cases were carried there by hand, not by wagon.

316
00:13:44,693 --> 00:13:47,420
Everyone else went by ambulance to Frederick,

317
00:13:47,420 --> 00:13:50,813
Maryland, and then by train to Baltimore and Washington.

318
00:13:51,263 --> 00:13:54,980
By October 30, the hospitals of Frederick held more

319
00:13:54,980 --> 00:13:56,438
than 5,000 patients.

320
00:13:56,888 --> 00:13:59,153
The doctors had paid a price too.

321
00:13:59,153 --> 00:14:02,380
Letterman wrote that some medical officers lost

322
00:14:02,380 --> 00:14:05,812
their lives at Antietam in their devotion to duty,

323
00:14:05,812 --> 00:14:08,764
among them an assistant surgeon of the 20th

324
00:14:08,764 --> 00:14:11,167
Massachusetts, killed on the field.

325
00:14:11,617 --> 00:14:14,779
Families arrived to take their badly wounded sons home.

326
00:14:14,779 --> 00:14:17,539
Letterman thought the journey often killed them.

327
00:14:17,539 --> 00:14:20,184
It was impossible, he wrote, to convince them.

328
00:14:20,634 --> 00:14:23,060
Antietam taught Letterman a second lesson.

329
00:14:23,060 --> 00:14:25,948
Getting a man off the field was only half the job.

330
00:14:27,548 --> 00:14:30,512
On October 30, 1862, he issued a circular.

331
00:14:30,512 --> 00:14:34,111
Before every battle, each army corps would set up a

332
00:14:34,111 --> 00:14:36,016
hospital for each division.

333
00:14:36,466 --> 00:14:39,619
In each one, three surgeons would do the operating.

334
00:14:39,619 --> 00:14:42,834
They were to be selected without regard to rank, but

335
00:14:42,834 --> 00:14:45,986
solely on account of their known prudence, judgment

336
00:14:45,986 --> 00:14:46,605
and skill.

337
00:14:47,055 --> 00:14:50,175
In doubtful cases, the three would consult together,

338
00:14:50,175 --> 00:14:53,475
and a majority would decide whether to operate and how.

339
00:14:53,925 --> 00:14:56,220
One assistant was to give the anaesthetic.

340
00:14:56,220 --> 00:14:59,390
Others would dress wounds, keep records, and feed the men.

341
00:14:59,840 --> 00:15:01,598
Every case was written down.

342
00:15:01,598 --> 00:15:04,863
And every man who died was buried in a marked grave,

343
00:15:04,863 --> 00:15:08,127
with a head-board giving his name, rank, company and

344
00:15:08,127 --> 00:15:08,692
regiment.

345
00:15:09,142 --> 00:15:10,896
Now the chain was complete.

346
00:15:10,896 --> 00:15:14,210
Just behind each regiment, a medical officer set up

347
00:15:14,210 --> 00:15:16,029
a small depot for first aid.

348
00:15:16,479 --> 00:15:19,283
From there, the ambulances carried men back to the

349
00:15:19,283 --> 00:15:20,292
division hospital.

350
00:15:20,292 --> 00:15:22,871
And from the division hospital, the trains and

351
00:15:22,871 --> 00:15:25,842
steamers carried them to the great general hospitals.

352
00:15:26,292 --> 00:15:29,299
At the field hospital, the surgeons had to choose.

353
00:15:29,299 --> 00:15:32,065
Historians at the National Museum of Civil War

354
00:15:32,065 --> 00:15:33,930
Medicine describe three groups:

355
00:15:33,930 --> 00:15:36,876
men who needed an operation now, men whose wounds

356
00:15:36,876 --> 00:15:39,883
were slight and could wait, and men believed to be

357
00:15:39,883 --> 00:15:40,905
mortally wounded.

358
00:15:41,355 --> 00:15:43,386
Letterman never used the word triage.

359
00:15:43,386 --> 00:15:45,253
But historians see its roots here.

360
00:15:45,703 --> 00:15:49,081
The first great test of the full system came at

361
00:15:49,081 --> 00:15:51,454
Fredericksburg, in December 1862.

362
00:15:51,904 --> 00:15:54,916
On the night of December 13, the ambulance men

363
00:15:54,916 --> 00:15:56,946
searched the field in the dark.

364
00:15:56,946 --> 00:15:58,911
They could not carry lanterns.

365
00:15:58,911 --> 00:16:02,317
The glimmer of a candle, Letterman wrote, invariably

366
00:16:02,317 --> 00:16:04,936
called forth a shot from a sharpshooter.

367
00:16:05,386 --> 00:16:06,726
They groped their way.

368
00:16:06,726 --> 00:16:09,892
And before dawn, all the wounded who were not beyond

369
00:16:09,892 --> 00:16:13,425
Union lines had been carried to the hospitals in the rear.

370
00:16:13,875 --> 00:16:17,032
Letterman later wrote that Fredericksburg was where

371
00:16:17,032 --> 00:16:19,757
his system was, for the first time, put into

372
00:16:19,757 --> 00:16:22,172
operation and severely tested. It held.

373
00:16:22,622 --> 00:16:25,819
So here is where you are now, if you were hit in

374
00:16:25,819 --> 00:16:26,619
that winter.

375
00:16:26,619 --> 00:16:28,617
Carried by men in green bands.

376
00:16:28,617 --> 00:16:31,348
Sorted by a surgeon chosen for his skill.

377
00:16:31,348 --> 00:16:34,812
Laid on a table in a division hospital, often within

378
00:16:34,812 --> 00:16:35,211
hours.

379
00:16:35,661 --> 00:16:38,373
In Washington, a few days later, a new volunteer

380
00:16:38,373 --> 00:16:40,802
nurse heard the shout through her hospital.

381
00:16:40,802 --> 00:16:42,327
They've come, they've come.

382
00:16:42,327 --> 00:16:44,134
Hurry up, ladies, you're wanted.

383
00:16:44,584 --> 00:16:46,393
Her name was Louisa May Alcott.

384
00:16:46,393 --> 00:16:48,669
The Fredericksburg wounded had arrived.

385
00:16:48,669 --> 00:16:51,236
And for many of them, what came next was the

386
00:16:51,236 --> 00:16:52,170
operating table.

387
00:16:52,620 --> 00:16:55,515
Her 40-bed ward mixed everything together.

388
00:16:55,515 --> 00:16:59,030
Pneumonia on one side, she wrote, diphtheria on the

389
00:16:59,030 --> 00:17:01,580
other, five typhoids on the opposite.

390
00:17:02,030 --> 00:17:04,094
Here is the first thing the movies get wrong.

391
00:17:05,694 --> 00:17:07,439
So now you are on the table.

392
00:17:07,439 --> 00:17:10,556
Picture a door laid across two barrels, or a plank

393
00:17:10,556 --> 00:17:13,112
in a barn, under whatever light there is.

394
00:17:13,112 --> 00:17:15,979
A surgeon in a stained coat looks down at you.

395
00:17:15,979 --> 00:17:17,101
What happens next?

396
00:17:17,551 --> 00:17:20,526
You have probably heard that Civil War soldiers bit

397
00:17:20,526 --> 00:17:23,383
on a bullet while the surgeon worked, wide awake.

398
00:17:23,383 --> 00:17:26,066
For the Union army, the records say otherwise.

399
00:17:26,516 --> 00:17:30,097
The Army's official medical history estimated that

400
00:17:30,097 --> 00:17:33,822
anaesthetics were used in no less than 80,000 cases.

401
00:17:33,822 --> 00:17:35,613
It called that a minimum.

402
00:17:36,063 --> 00:17:40,015
The same records list only 254 cases where surgeons

403
00:17:40,015 --> 00:17:42,882
stated that no anaesthetic was given.

404
00:17:43,332 --> 00:17:46,947
A 2014 article in the journal Military Medicine put

405
00:17:46,947 --> 00:17:47,727
it bluntly:

406
00:17:47,727 --> 00:17:51,058
this should put to rest the stories of soldiers

407
00:17:51,058 --> 00:17:52,334
biting on bullets.

408
00:17:52,784 --> 00:17:55,272
The drug of choice was chloroform.

409
00:17:55,272 --> 00:17:58,858
In 8,900 operations where the agent was recorded,

410
00:17:58,858 --> 00:18:02,444
chloroform was used in about three of every four.

411
00:18:02,894 --> 00:18:03,846
Why chloroform?

412
00:18:03,846 --> 00:18:06,133
The Army's history gave two reasons:

413
00:18:06,133 --> 00:18:09,373
the rapidity of its effects, and the small quantity

414
00:18:09,373 --> 00:18:09,945
required.

415
00:18:09,945 --> 00:18:11,978
A modern historian adds a third.

416
00:18:11,978 --> 00:18:15,090
Unlike ether, it did not catch fire, near candles

417
00:18:15,090 --> 00:18:15,916
and lanterns.

418
00:18:16,366 --> 00:18:17,725
It went on a cloth.

419
00:18:17,725 --> 00:18:21,158
A Confederate surgeon's manual of 1861 advised a

420
00:18:21,158 --> 00:18:24,591
folded cloth in the form of a cone, with a small

421
00:18:24,591 --> 00:18:26,522
piece of sponge in the tip.

422
00:18:26,972 --> 00:18:29,304
The cone went over your nose and mouth.

423
00:18:29,304 --> 00:18:32,114
On average, one study found, it took about nine

424
00:18:32,114 --> 00:18:33,489
minutes to take effect.

425
00:18:33,939 --> 00:18:36,897
Field surgeons used chloroform almost uniformly.

426
00:18:36,897 --> 00:18:40,041
In the big city hospitals, where there was time and

427
00:18:40,041 --> 00:18:41,828
space, ether was more common.

428
00:18:42,278 --> 00:18:44,345
Some men fought it as it took hold.

429
00:18:44,345 --> 00:18:47,002
Others feared it would not reach them at all.

430
00:18:47,002 --> 00:18:49,659
One soldier, the National Museum of Civil War

431
00:18:49,659 --> 00:18:52,553
Medicine records, was afraid the chloroform would

432
00:18:52,553 --> 00:18:54,797
give out before it got to be his turn.

433
00:18:55,247 --> 00:18:57,984
Afterwards, relief was harder to find.

434
00:18:57,984 --> 00:19:01,370
One wounded private remembered asking a surgeon

435
00:19:01,370 --> 00:19:05,188
whether he had any chloroform, or quinine, or whisky.

436
00:19:05,188 --> 00:19:06,485
The answer was no.

437
00:19:06,935 --> 00:19:08,683
It was not without risk.

438
00:19:08,683 --> 00:19:12,250
Among those 8,900 recorded operations, the Army's

439
00:19:12,250 --> 00:19:16,036
history counted 43 deaths blamed on the anaesthetic.

440
00:19:16,036 --> 00:19:19,021
For chloroform, about five in a thousand.

441
00:19:19,471 --> 00:19:21,184
Surgeons argued about it.

442
00:19:21,184 --> 00:19:24,816
One complained of its extravagant and indiscreet use.

443
00:19:24,816 --> 00:19:25,776
Another wrote:

444
00:19:25,776 --> 00:19:29,202
on the field of battle, chloroform is the safe and

445
00:19:29,202 --> 00:19:30,367
preferable agent.

446
00:19:30,817 --> 00:19:34,236
Twenty years later, the Army's verdict was settled.

447
00:19:34,236 --> 00:19:37,521
The inestimable value of anaesthetics in military

448
00:19:37,521 --> 00:19:41,140
surgery, it wrote, will hardly be denied at this date.

449
00:19:41,590 --> 00:19:44,251
So when the surgeon reached for the saw, you were

450
00:19:44,251 --> 00:19:45,283
very likely asleep.

451
00:19:45,283 --> 00:19:47,998
What happened next was the second thing the movies

452
00:19:47,998 --> 00:19:48,541
get wrong.

453
00:19:48,991 --> 00:19:50,134
The amputation.

454
00:19:51,734 --> 00:19:55,767
Union surgeons recorded 29,980 amputations.

455
00:19:55,767 --> 00:20:00,362
Counting both armies, historians put the total at

456
00:20:00,362 --> 00:20:02,707
perhaps 50,000 to 60,000.

457
00:20:03,157 --> 00:20:05,900
It is easy to call the surgeons butchers.

458
00:20:05,900 --> 00:20:07,439
Most historians do not.

459
00:20:07,439 --> 00:20:08,844
Here is why they cut.

460
00:20:09,294 --> 00:20:12,427
The Minié ball splintered bone and drove dirt, cloth

461
00:20:12,427 --> 00:20:13,993
and debris into the wound.

462
00:20:13,993 --> 00:20:16,524
When the nerves and vessels of a limb were

463
00:20:16,524 --> 00:20:19,837
destroyed, amputation gave the best chance of survival.

464
00:20:20,287 --> 00:20:23,580
The Army's medical museum says amputation made a

465
00:20:23,580 --> 00:20:25,021
complex wound simple.

466
00:20:25,021 --> 00:20:28,589
And with hundreds of men waiting, it was the fastest

467
00:20:28,589 --> 00:20:32,157
way to treat the largest number of wounded in a very

468
00:20:32,157 --> 00:20:33,598
short window of time.

469
00:20:34,048 --> 00:20:37,357
An experienced surgeon could finish an amputation in

470
00:20:37,357 --> 00:20:38,947
roughly 10 to 15 minutes.

471
00:20:39,397 --> 00:20:41,878
Surgical manuals taught that it should be done

472
00:20:41,878 --> 00:20:43,280
within the first two days.

473
00:20:43,280 --> 00:20:44,898
The records proved them right.

474
00:20:45,348 --> 00:20:48,619
Amputations done in the first 48 hours killed about

475
00:20:48,619 --> 00:20:49,388
one in four.

476
00:20:49,388 --> 00:20:51,954
Those done in the days that followed, as

477
00:20:51,954 --> 00:20:55,225
inflammation set in, killed more than one in three.

478
00:20:55,225 --> 00:20:57,855
The worst time to operate was in between.

479
00:20:58,305 --> 00:21:01,682
So a surgeon who cut quickly was often saving a life.

480
00:21:01,682 --> 00:21:04,677
Trying to save the limb by cutting out only the

481
00:21:04,677 --> 00:21:07,544
shattered bone, the records show, was usually

482
00:21:07,544 --> 00:21:09,137
deadlier than amputation.

483
00:21:09,587 --> 00:21:13,143
Overall, of every hundred Union amputations, about

484
00:21:13,143 --> 00:21:14,992
twenty-six ended in death.

485
00:21:15,442 --> 00:21:18,931
Turn that around, and the National Park Service puts

486
00:21:18,931 --> 00:21:19,602
it simply:

487
00:21:19,602 --> 00:21:23,092
about three in four soldiers survived an amputation.

488
00:21:23,092 --> 00:21:25,038
In 1862, that was a good bet.

489
00:21:25,488 --> 00:21:28,363
And the closer the cut was to the body, the deadlier

490
00:21:28,363 --> 00:21:28,750
it was.

491
00:21:29,200 --> 00:21:32,665
Lose a hand or fingers, and about three in a hundred died.

492
00:21:32,665 --> 00:21:34,398
At the thigh, more than half.

493
00:21:34,398 --> 00:21:37,265
At the hip joint, sixty-six cases were recorded.

494
00:21:37,265 --> 00:21:38,998
Fifty-five of those men died.

495
00:21:39,448 --> 00:21:42,714
Not every surgeon was skilled, and soldiers knew it.

496
00:21:42,714 --> 00:21:45,729
A Confederate veteran named John Opie wrote that

497
00:21:45,729 --> 00:21:48,807
many limbs were lost that should have been saved.

498
00:21:49,257 --> 00:21:53,007
But the surgeons themselves were worn to the bone.

499
00:21:53,007 --> 00:21:56,532
In May 1864, a Massachusetts surgeon named John

500
00:21:56,532 --> 00:21:57,432
Perry wrote:

501
00:21:57,432 --> 00:22:01,032
I am so exhausted and nervous it is difficult to

502
00:22:01,032 --> 00:22:02,157
express myself.

503
00:22:02,157 --> 00:22:04,182
Am operating day and night.

504
00:22:04,632 --> 00:22:07,690
Every day there is a fight, he wrote, and every day

505
00:22:07,690 --> 00:22:09,429
the hospital is again filled.

506
00:22:09,879 --> 00:22:12,336
Even famous patients went under the saw.

507
00:22:12,336 --> 00:22:15,284
General Daniel Sickles lost a leg at Gettysburg.

508
00:22:15,284 --> 00:22:18,417
His amputation healed fast, and he was back on duty

509
00:22:18,417 --> 00:22:19,277
within months.

510
00:22:19,277 --> 00:22:22,287
He later gave the leg to the Army Medical Museum.

511
00:22:22,737 --> 00:22:25,954
You have survived the bullet, the field, the cone

512
00:22:25,954 --> 00:22:26,742
and the saw.

513
00:22:26,742 --> 00:22:30,024
Now comes the enemy that nobody in 1862 could see.

514
00:22:30,474 --> 00:22:31,282
Infection.

515
00:22:32,882 --> 00:22:36,372
Years later, a Union surgeon named William Williams

516
00:22:36,372 --> 00:22:37,877
Keen told it straight.

517
00:22:37,877 --> 00:22:41,298
In the Civil War, surgeons knew absolutely nothing

518
00:22:41,298 --> 00:22:41,914
of germs.

519
00:22:42,364 --> 00:22:45,468
They operated, he said, in old blood-stained coats,

520
00:22:45,468 --> 00:22:47,477
the veterans of a hundred fights.

521
00:22:47,927 --> 00:22:51,006
Sponges were reused from one patient to the next,

522
00:22:51,006 --> 00:22:52,577
washed only in tap water.

523
00:22:52,577 --> 00:22:55,844
If an instrument fell on the floor, it was rinsed in

524
00:22:55,844 --> 00:22:57,352
a basin, and used again.

525
00:22:57,802 --> 00:23:00,722
When a wound filled with thick, creamy pus, surgeons

526
00:23:00,722 --> 00:23:01,452
were pleased.

527
00:23:01,452 --> 00:23:04,372
In their records they called it laudable pus, a sign

528
00:23:04,372 --> 00:23:04,989
of healing.

529
00:23:05,439 --> 00:23:08,627
They recognized four deadly wound infections:

530
00:23:08,627 --> 00:23:12,240
hospital gangrene, erysipelas, pyemia, and tetanus.

531
00:23:12,690 --> 00:23:17,244
Pyemia, what doctors then called blood poisoning,

532
00:23:17,244 --> 00:23:19,846
was almost a death sentence.

533
00:23:19,846 --> 00:23:23,657
Of 2,818 recorded cases, 71 men survived.

534
00:23:24,107 --> 00:23:26,991
In established cases, the Army admitted, all

535
00:23:26,991 --> 00:23:29,089
remedies were found ineffective.

536
00:23:29,539 --> 00:23:31,104
Tetanus was rare:

537
00:23:31,104 --> 00:23:34,879
about two cases in every thousand wounds.

538
00:23:34,879 --> 00:23:38,285
But of 505 cases, 451 ended in death.

539
00:23:38,735 --> 00:23:41,573
Blood vessel surgery was grim too.

540
00:23:41,573 --> 00:23:45,662
Keen later counted 374 ties of the main artery of

541
00:23:45,662 --> 00:23:46,496
the thigh.

542
00:23:46,496 --> 00:23:48,332
281 of those men died.

543
00:23:48,782 --> 00:23:51,999
Of men recorded with gangrene after gunshot wounds,

544
00:23:51,999 --> 00:23:55,152
nearly half died, though often of the wound itself

545
00:23:55,152 --> 00:23:56,602
or other complications.

546
00:23:56,602 --> 00:24:00,197
It was worst in crowded city hospitals, later in the war.

547
00:24:00,647 --> 00:24:03,668
And yet, some surgeons were learning by watching.

548
00:24:03,668 --> 00:24:06,565
A Union surgeon named Middleton Goldsmith began

549
00:24:06,565 --> 00:24:09,524
treating hospital gangrene with bromine, applied

550
00:24:09,524 --> 00:24:10,819
directly to the sore.

551
00:24:11,269 --> 00:24:14,670
In his own series, only eight of 304 patients died.

552
00:24:14,670 --> 00:24:17,338
His results were not a controlled trial.

553
00:24:17,338 --> 00:24:20,607
But one case note recorded that bromine had acted

554
00:24:20,607 --> 00:24:21,474
like a charm.

555
00:24:21,924 --> 00:24:24,136
Letterman noticed something too.

556
00:24:24,136 --> 00:24:27,662
At Antietam, men treated in the open air did better

557
00:24:27,662 --> 00:24:28,907
than men in barns.

558
00:24:28,907 --> 00:24:32,156
And men in barns did better than men in houses.

559
00:24:32,606 --> 00:24:33,719
Fresh air helped.

560
00:24:33,719 --> 00:24:35,028
Nobody yet knew why.

561
00:24:35,028 --> 00:24:38,367
The answer came two years after the war ended, when

562
00:24:38,367 --> 00:24:41,443
a British surgeon, Joseph Lister, published his

563
00:24:41,443 --> 00:24:43,996
antiseptic method, using carbolic acid.

564
00:24:44,446 --> 00:24:47,347
For the Civil War soldier, it came too late.

565
00:24:47,347 --> 00:24:50,446
And in the summer of 1863, the war produced its

566
00:24:50,446 --> 00:24:52,358
largest crowd of wounded yet.

567
00:24:52,808 --> 00:24:53,678
Gettysburg.

568
00:24:55,278 --> 00:24:59,393
Three days of fighting in southern Pennsylvania,

569
00:24:59,393 --> 00:25:00,679
July 1–3, 1863.

570
00:25:01,129 --> 00:25:05,867
Letterman counted the cost for his department.

571
00:25:05,867 --> 00:25:08,031
14,193 Union wounded.

572
00:25:08,031 --> 00:25:12,563
6,802 Confederate wounded left in his hands.

573
00:25:12,563 --> 00:25:14,417
Nearly 21,000 men.

574
00:25:14,867 --> 00:25:17,534
This time the ambulance corps was ready.

575
00:25:17,534 --> 00:25:20,935
Before daylight on July 4, every wounded man within

576
00:25:20,935 --> 00:25:24,336
Union lines, about 12,000 on the last two days, had

577
00:25:24,336 --> 00:25:26,337
been carried to the hospitals.

578
00:25:26,787 --> 00:25:29,739
I know of no battlefield, Letterman reported, from

579
00:25:29,739 --> 00:25:32,455
which wounded men have been so speedily and so

580
00:25:32,455 --> 00:25:33,518
carefully removed.

581
00:25:33,968 --> 00:25:36,912
Letterman himself never forgot what he saw there.

582
00:25:36,912 --> 00:25:39,737
It was a field of blood, he wrote, on which the

583
00:25:39,737 --> 00:25:41,540
demon of destruction revelled.

584
00:25:41,990 --> 00:25:43,950
The ambulance men paid for it.

585
00:25:43,950 --> 00:25:47,348
At Gettysburg, an ambulance officer and two privates

586
00:25:47,348 --> 00:25:49,505
were killed, 17 privates wounded.

587
00:25:49,955 --> 00:25:53,287
A lieutenant of the 61st New York, Charles Fuller,

588
00:25:53,287 --> 00:25:55,020
was hit in the Wheatfield.

589
00:25:55,020 --> 00:25:58,153
Lying under fire, he saw two men heading to the

590
00:25:58,153 --> 00:26:01,019
rear, and yelled out to them: drag me back.

591
00:26:01,469 --> 00:26:04,323
Later, he wrote, I was taken off the table and put

592
00:26:04,323 --> 00:26:07,520
back on my stretcher, which was set down in a wall tent.

593
00:26:07,970 --> 00:26:11,008
Fuller had lain in the open before the bearers

594
00:26:11,008 --> 00:26:11,801
reached him.

595
00:26:11,801 --> 00:26:14,509
His memoir names that time in four words:

596
00:26:14,509 --> 00:26:16,887
while lying here, entirely helpless.

597
00:26:17,337 --> 00:26:21,267
An officer of the 7th Wisconsin wrote thirty years later:

598
00:26:21,267 --> 00:26:24,852
I was shot in the right breast, the ball entering my

599
00:26:24,852 --> 00:26:26,852
lung, where it still remains.

600
00:26:27,302 --> 00:26:30,525
Houses and barns, but chiefly the woods, were used

601
00:26:30,525 --> 00:26:31,363
as hospitals.

602
00:26:31,363 --> 00:26:34,393
Historians count more than sixty field hospital

603
00:26:34,393 --> 00:26:35,811
sites around the town.

604
00:26:36,261 --> 00:26:37,734
But there was a problem.

605
00:26:37,734 --> 00:26:40,926
By army order, the medical wagons and tents had been

606
00:26:40,926 --> 00:26:43,075
sent to the rear before the battle.

607
00:26:43,075 --> 00:26:46,083
They could not reach the field in time to protect

608
00:26:46,083 --> 00:26:49,459
the wounded from the drenching rain that fell after it.

609
00:26:49,909 --> 00:26:52,610
Letterman was furious, in his precise way.

610
00:26:52,610 --> 00:26:55,439
Lost supplies, he wrote, can be replenished.

611
00:26:55,439 --> 00:26:57,497
But lives lost are gone forever.

612
00:26:57,947 --> 00:27:01,754
650 medical officers worked from the opening of the

613
00:27:01,754 --> 00:27:04,218
battle until the night of July 6.

614
00:27:04,668 --> 00:27:07,464
In one corps that kept its wagons, the medical

615
00:27:07,464 --> 00:27:10,563
director reported every man's wounds dressed within

616
00:27:10,563 --> 00:27:13,541
six hours after the battle ended, and every major

617
00:27:13,541 --> 00:27:16,762
operation done within twenty-four hours of the wound.

618
00:27:17,212 --> 00:27:20,214
His goal, in his own report, was to have every

619
00:27:20,214 --> 00:27:23,608
capital operation performed within twenty-four hours

620
00:27:23,608 --> 00:27:25,500
after the wound was received.

621
00:27:25,950 --> 00:27:29,633
A Pennsylvania private shot on the first day remembered:

622
00:27:29,633 --> 00:27:32,921
I lay in the clover field until the evening of the

623
00:27:32,921 --> 00:27:33,513
next day.

624
00:27:33,963 --> 00:27:37,406
A wounded Confederate officer, held as a prisoner,

625
00:27:37,406 --> 00:27:38,715
wrote in his diary:

626
00:27:38,715 --> 00:27:42,296
all night long these ambulances move among us, still

627
00:27:42,296 --> 00:27:43,880
bringing more and more.

628
00:27:44,330 --> 00:27:47,205
The whole ground for miles around, he wrote, is

629
00:27:47,205 --> 00:27:50,201
covered with the wounded, the dying and the dead.

630
00:27:50,651 --> 00:27:53,863
Then the army marched on, and left the town with its

631
00:27:53,863 --> 00:27:54,357
wounded.

632
00:27:54,357 --> 00:27:57,383
On a farm about a mile east of Gettysburg, a tent

633
00:27:57,383 --> 00:27:58,000
city rose:

634
00:27:58,000 --> 00:27:58,927
Camp Letterman.

635
00:27:59,377 --> 00:28:02,632
At its height it held more than 400 tents, each for

636
00:28:02,632 --> 00:28:04,036
up to twelve patients.

637
00:28:04,036 --> 00:28:07,291
The American Battlefield Trust calls it the largest

638
00:28:07,291 --> 00:28:09,334
tented hospital site of the war.

639
00:28:09,784 --> 00:28:11,847
Construction had begun on July 10.

640
00:28:11,847 --> 00:28:14,699
The Sanitary and Christian Commissions both ran

641
00:28:14,699 --> 00:28:15,791
posts at the camp.

642
00:28:16,241 --> 00:28:19,689
Roughly 4,000 of the most serious cases passed through it.

643
00:28:19,689 --> 00:28:22,543
On one August day, nearly half its patients were

644
00:28:22,543 --> 00:28:23,316
Confederates.

645
00:28:23,766 --> 00:28:26,472
A militia guard wrote home in October:

646
00:28:26,472 --> 00:28:28,821
as high as seventeen die per day.

647
00:28:29,271 --> 00:28:32,138
When the tents finally came down in November, a

648
00:28:32,138 --> 00:28:35,127
nurse named Sophronia Bucklin looked at the bare,

649
00:28:35,127 --> 00:28:38,298
trampled ground where the wards had stood, and wrote

650
00:28:38,298 --> 00:28:41,165
that each space marked where the dead had lain.

651
00:28:41,615 --> 00:28:45,116
Camp Letterman closed on November 20, 1863.

652
00:28:45,116 --> 00:28:48,780
The day before, nearby, President Lincoln had

653
00:28:48,780 --> 00:28:51,711
dedicated the new national cemetery.

654
00:28:52,161 --> 00:28:53,704
Today, the fields are quiet.

655
00:28:54,504 --> 00:28:57,147
For most wounded men, the last stop was a city.

656
00:28:58,747 --> 00:29:03,690
At its peak, the Union army ran 204 general

657
00:29:03,690 --> 00:29:07,024
hospitals, with 136,894 beds.

658
00:29:07,474 --> 00:29:10,056
Washington became a city of the wounded.

659
00:29:10,056 --> 00:29:13,349
Walt Whitman wrote that within sight of the Capitol

660
00:29:13,349 --> 00:29:16,642
stood some thirty or forty such hospitals, at times

661
00:29:16,642 --> 00:29:18,515
holding 50,000 to 70,000 men.

662
00:29:18,965 --> 00:29:21,257
The new ones were built as pavilions:

663
00:29:21,257 --> 00:29:24,480
long, narrow wards, with windows in facing pairs, so

664
00:29:24,480 --> 00:29:26,463
air could blow straight through.

665
00:29:26,463 --> 00:29:29,623
It was a lesson taken from Florence Nightingale and

666
00:29:29,623 --> 00:29:30,615
the Crimean War.

667
00:29:31,065 --> 00:29:35,352
Satterlee Hospital, in West Philadelphia, opened in

668
00:29:35,352 --> 00:29:38,463
June 1862, with more than 3,500 beds.

669
00:29:38,913 --> 00:29:42,497
Mower Hospital, in Chestnut Hill, had about 3,600.

670
00:29:42,497 --> 00:29:46,226
Inside, the newer hospitals did something that seems

671
00:29:46,226 --> 00:29:47,086
obvious now:

672
00:29:47,086 --> 00:29:49,667
they divided their wards by disease.

673
00:29:50,117 --> 00:29:52,000
Inside, the wards were long and bright.

674
00:29:52,450 --> 00:29:54,603
Getting there was its own journey.

675
00:29:54,603 --> 00:29:58,277
More than 25,000 men were carried north by hospital train.

676
00:29:58,727 --> 00:30:02,082
In one wartime sketch of a hospital car, wounded men

677
00:30:02,082 --> 00:30:05,438
lie in bunks stacked in tiers along both walls, with

678
00:30:05,438 --> 00:30:07,696
an attendant bent over one of them.

679
00:30:08,146 --> 00:30:11,652
Hospital ships were refitted deck by deck.

680
00:30:11,652 --> 00:30:13,237
The steamship J. K.

681
00:30:13,237 --> 00:30:17,327
Barnes, fitted out in New York in 1864, held more

682
00:30:17,327 --> 00:30:18,579
than 350 bunks.

683
00:30:19,029 --> 00:30:23,549
On the rivers, hospital steamers did the same. The D. A.

684
00:30:23,549 --> 00:30:25,810
January had nearly 450 beds.

685
00:30:25,810 --> 00:30:30,250
In four years, it carried more than 23,000 wounded men.

686
00:30:30,700 --> 00:30:32,194
And the Navy had its own.

687
00:30:32,194 --> 00:30:35,240
The side-wheel steamer Red Rover, captured from the

688
00:30:35,240 --> 00:30:38,347
Confederates, became the Navy's first hospital ship.

689
00:30:38,797 --> 00:30:41,930
It carried an operating room, a laundry, an elevator

690
00:30:41,930 --> 00:30:42,773
and bathrooms.

691
00:30:42,773 --> 00:30:45,786
More than two thousand patients passed through it.

692
00:30:45,786 --> 00:30:48,738
Catholic Sisters of the Holy Cross nursed aboard,

693
00:30:48,738 --> 00:30:51,509
among the first women to serve on a Navy ship.

694
00:30:51,959 --> 00:30:54,994
In the hospitals, the nurses were waiting.

695
00:30:54,994 --> 00:30:58,463
Dorothea Dix, Superintendent of Army Nurses from

696
00:30:58,463 --> 00:31:01,281
June 1861, wanted women who were older,

697
00:31:01,281 --> 00:31:05,328
plain-looking and plainly dressed. No jewelry. No curls.

698
00:31:05,328 --> 00:31:06,412
No hoop skirts.

699
00:31:06,862 --> 00:31:09,252
Her nurses nicknamed her Dragon Dix.

700
00:31:09,252 --> 00:31:12,573
Thousands of women served as official Army nurses,

701
00:31:12,573 --> 00:31:15,826
and more than 20,000 worked in Union hospitals in

702
00:31:15,826 --> 00:31:16,490
some role.

703
00:31:16,940 --> 00:31:19,136
Dix asked for no salary herself.

704
00:31:19,136 --> 00:31:21,469
She wrote to the Secretary of War:

705
00:31:21,469 --> 00:31:24,969
I give cheerfully my whole time, mind, strength and

706
00:31:24,969 --> 00:31:27,508
income, to the service of my country.

707
00:31:27,958 --> 00:31:31,324
Louisa May Alcott nursed only a few weeks before

708
00:31:31,324 --> 00:31:33,569
typhoid pneumonia sent her home.

709
00:31:33,569 --> 00:31:37,076
She remembered the smell of her ward as a regiment

710
00:31:37,076 --> 00:31:40,933
of the vilest odors that ever assaulted the human nose.

711
00:31:41,383 --> 00:31:44,273
In the West, a widow from Illinois named Mary Ann

712
00:31:44,273 --> 00:31:47,162
Bickerdyke cooked, cleaned and fought officers on

713
00:31:47,162 --> 00:31:48,223
behalf of the men.

714
00:31:48,223 --> 00:31:50,228
They called her Mother Bickerdyke.

715
00:31:50,678 --> 00:31:53,533
And in Washington, a poet came almost every day.

716
00:31:53,533 --> 00:31:56,923
Walt Whitman brought paper, fruit, tobacco, and his time.

717
00:31:57,373 --> 00:32:01,368
By his own estimate, he made over 600 visits, among

718
00:32:01,368 --> 00:32:04,422
80,000 to 100,000 wounded and sick men.

719
00:32:04,872 --> 00:32:08,224
He called those years the greatest privilege of his life.

720
00:32:08,224 --> 00:32:11,165
And he warned that the real war would never get in

721
00:32:11,165 --> 00:32:11,753
the books.

722
00:32:12,203 --> 00:32:15,364
For a wounded man who made it here, the odds were

723
00:32:15,364 --> 00:32:17,815
better than anywhere else he had been.

724
00:32:17,815 --> 00:32:20,847
More than a million cases were treated in Union

725
00:32:20,847 --> 00:32:22,008
general hospitals.

726
00:32:22,008 --> 00:32:25,169
About eight in every hundred died, remarkably low

727
00:32:25,169 --> 00:32:26,008
for the time.

728
00:32:26,458 --> 00:32:29,468
But many of those million cases had nothing to do

729
00:32:29,468 --> 00:32:30,267
with bullets.

730
00:32:30,717 --> 00:32:34,044
The biggest killer of the Civil War did not carry a rifle.

731
00:32:35,644 --> 00:32:41,204
By the Army's own count in 1870, 186,216 Union

732
00:32:41,204 --> 00:32:44,709
soldiers had died of disease.

733
00:32:44,709 --> 00:32:49,907
Killed in battle or dead of wounds: 93,443.

734
00:32:50,357 --> 00:32:53,818
For every Union soldier killed by the enemy, roughly

735
00:32:53,818 --> 00:32:55,216
two died of sickness.

736
00:32:55,666 --> 00:32:58,137
The worst was diarrhea and dysentery.

737
00:32:58,137 --> 00:32:59,873
Well over a million cases.

738
00:32:59,873 --> 00:33:01,476
More than 44,000 deaths.

739
00:33:01,926 --> 00:33:03,581
Typhoid followed.

740
00:33:03,581 --> 00:33:09,033
148,000 cases in Union troops, and nearly 35,000 deaths.

741
00:33:09,483 --> 00:33:14,475
Smallpox killed 7,058 of 18,952 Union soldiers who

742
00:33:14,475 --> 00:33:19,367
caught it, even though a vaccine already existed.

743
00:33:19,817 --> 00:33:24,868
The camp fevers, typhoid and its cousins, killed

744
00:33:24,868 --> 00:33:30,341
35,965 white soldiers and 4,691 soldiers of the U.S.

745
00:33:30,341 --> 00:33:31,919
Colored Troops.

746
00:33:32,369 --> 00:33:35,702
Measles hit hardest in new recruits from farms, boys

747
00:33:35,702 --> 00:33:38,459
who had never met crowds or crowd diseases.

748
00:33:38,459 --> 00:33:40,831
In camp, they met everything at once.

749
00:33:41,281 --> 00:33:43,685
Pneumonia killed almost 20,000 more.

750
00:33:44,135 --> 00:33:48,976
Today the National Park Service gives the Union's

751
00:33:48,976 --> 00:33:54,113
totals as 110,100 killed in battle, and 224,580 dead

752
00:33:54,113 --> 00:33:55,199
of disease.

753
00:33:55,649 --> 00:33:57,307
And the whole war?

754
00:33:57,307 --> 00:34:02,095
For more than a century, the dead of both sides were

755
00:34:02,095 --> 00:34:04,582
estimated at about 620,000.

756
00:34:04,582 --> 00:34:06,515
In 2011, historian J.

757
00:34:06,515 --> 00:34:11,120
David Hacker used census records to estimate about

758
00:34:11,120 --> 00:34:11,856
750,000.

759
00:34:12,306 --> 00:34:14,032
Which brings us to the men who lived.

760
00:34:14,482 --> 00:34:16,871
The war ended in the spring of 1865.

761
00:34:16,871 --> 00:34:19,460
For the wounded, it did not end at all.

762
00:34:21,060 --> 00:34:24,341
If you survived all of it, the bullet, the field,

763
00:34:24,341 --> 00:34:26,618
the saw, the fever, you went home.

764
00:34:26,618 --> 00:34:30,101
Often to a farm, often with one arm or one leg, to a

765
00:34:30,101 --> 00:34:33,316
country that had never seen so many wounded men.

766
00:34:33,766 --> 00:34:37,459
Just under 30,000 Union soldiers lost a limb during

767
00:34:37,459 --> 00:34:38,038
the war.

768
00:34:38,038 --> 00:34:40,501
More than 21,000 of them survived.

769
00:34:40,951 --> 00:34:44,764
The American Battlefield Trust estimates that one in

770
00:34:44,764 --> 00:34:49,017
13 surviving soldiers came home missing one or more limbs.

771
00:34:49,467 --> 00:34:52,957
Washington paid for an artificial limb, or up to $75

772
00:34:52,957 --> 00:34:53,494
instead.

773
00:34:53,494 --> 00:34:56,783
Confederate veterans got nothing from the federal

774
00:34:56,783 --> 00:34:59,803
government, so Southern states, such as North

775
00:34:59,803 --> 00:35:03,092
Carolina and Virginia, ran programs of their own.

776
00:35:03,542 --> 00:35:06,787
Many veterans who had lost an arm refused the hook.

777
00:35:06,787 --> 00:35:08,759
They preferred an empty sleeve.

778
00:35:09,209 --> 00:35:12,319
Men who had lost their right arms learned to write

779
00:35:12,319 --> 00:35:13,314
with their left.

780
00:35:13,314 --> 00:35:16,548
A New Yorker named William Oland Bourne ran contests

781
00:35:16,548 --> 00:35:17,854
for their penmanship.

782
00:35:17,854 --> 00:35:19,472
270 men entered the first.

783
00:35:19,922 --> 00:35:24,187
A law of July 1862 gave a totally disabled private

784
00:35:24,187 --> 00:35:25,126
$8 a month.

785
00:35:25,126 --> 00:35:29,733
The loss of a limb always counted as total disability.

786
00:35:29,733 --> 00:35:34,169
By 1885, the nation was paying $36 million a year in

787
00:35:34,169 --> 00:35:34,937
pensions.

788
00:35:35,387 --> 00:35:37,313
And Clara Barton kept working.

789
00:35:37,313 --> 00:35:40,458
After the war she ran an office that searched for

790
00:35:40,458 --> 00:35:43,538
missing soldiers, and helped reconnect more than

791
00:35:43,538 --> 00:35:45,785
20,000 of them with their families.

792
00:35:45,785 --> 00:35:48,609
In 1881, she founded the American Red Cross.

793
00:35:49,059 --> 00:35:50,292
Nursing changed too.

794
00:35:50,292 --> 00:35:53,254
After the war, the nursing leader Mary Livermore

795
00:35:53,254 --> 00:35:55,968
praised how far it had come as a profession.

796
00:35:56,418 --> 00:35:59,154
Artificial limbs became an industry.

797
00:35:59,154 --> 00:36:02,726
Between 1845 and 1861, only 34 patents had been

798
00:36:02,726 --> 00:36:03,942
issued for them.

799
00:36:03,942 --> 00:36:07,893
After the war, inventors raced to build better ones.

800
00:36:08,343 --> 00:36:10,248
Medicine kept something too.

801
00:36:10,248 --> 00:36:13,580
In 1862, Surgeon General Hammond founded the Army

802
00:36:13,580 --> 00:36:14,601
Medical Museum.

803
00:36:14,601 --> 00:36:17,661
Surgeons were ordered to collect and preserve

804
00:36:17,661 --> 00:36:18,818
specimens for it.

805
00:36:19,268 --> 00:36:22,536
Its purpose, in a phrase quoted by the National

806
00:36:22,536 --> 00:36:24,553
Museum of Civil War Medicine:

807
00:36:24,553 --> 00:36:27,822
to preserve, for future application, the dearly

808
00:36:27,822 --> 00:36:30,535
bought experience of four years of war.

809
00:36:30,985 --> 00:36:33,827
It still exists, as the National Museum of Health

810
00:36:33,827 --> 00:36:34,581
and Medicine.

811
00:36:35,031 --> 00:36:38,272
The Surgeon General's office also built up a library

812
00:36:38,272 --> 00:36:40,890
of medical books during and after the war.

813
00:36:40,890 --> 00:36:44,505
It grew into what is now the National Library of Medicine.

814
00:36:44,955 --> 00:36:48,460
And the clerks filled more than 16,000 folio volumes

815
00:36:48,460 --> 00:36:49,943
of manuscript records:

816
00:36:49,943 --> 00:36:53,717
who was hit, where, what was done, and whether he lived.

817
00:36:54,167 --> 00:36:56,203
And the Army wrote it all down.

818
00:36:56,203 --> 00:36:59,488
The Medical and Surgical History of the War of the

819
00:36:59,488 --> 00:37:02,838
Rebellion was published in six volumes between 1870

820
00:37:02,838 --> 00:37:03,429
and 1888.

821
00:37:03,429 --> 00:37:05,137
Thousands of case reports.

822
00:37:05,137 --> 00:37:08,159
Many of the numbers in this film come from it.

823
00:37:08,609 --> 00:37:10,955
And Letterman's ambulance corps?

824
00:37:10,955 --> 00:37:14,182
On March 11, 1864, Congress passed an act to

825
00:37:14,182 --> 00:37:17,628
establish a uniform system of ambulances in the

826
00:37:17,628 --> 00:37:19,681
armies of the United States.

827
00:37:20,131 --> 00:37:23,834
Ambulances were to be used only for the sick and wounded.

828
00:37:23,834 --> 00:37:26,562
The Army's history says the basic plan was

829
00:37:26,562 --> 00:37:28,186
unmistakably Letterman's.

830
00:37:28,636 --> 00:37:31,210
Letterman resigned in December 1864.

831
00:37:31,210 --> 00:37:34,356
He died in 1872, and is buried at Arlington.

832
00:37:34,356 --> 00:37:37,574
Today he is often called the father of modern

833
00:37:37,574 --> 00:37:39,076
battlefield medicine.

834
00:37:39,526 --> 00:37:42,584
His gravestone praises the man who brought order and

835
00:37:42,584 --> 00:37:44,701
efficiency into the Medical Service.

836
00:37:44,701 --> 00:37:46,348
That is exactly what he did.

837
00:37:46,798 --> 00:37:49,975
In 1911, the Army hospital at the Presidio of San

838
00:37:49,975 --> 00:37:53,153
Francisco was renamed Letterman General Hospital.

839
00:37:53,153 --> 00:37:56,654
It carried his name for most of the twentieth century.

840
00:37:57,104 --> 00:38:00,413
Eighty years later, the chief surgeon of the U.S.

841
00:38:00,413 --> 00:38:03,721
Army in Europe in World War II, Paul Hawley, said

842
00:38:03,721 --> 00:38:06,895
there was not a day in that war that he did not

843
00:38:06,895 --> 00:38:09,123
thank God for Jonathan Letterman.

844
00:38:09,573 --> 00:38:11,108
His idea is still here.

845
00:38:11,108 --> 00:38:14,512
The chain he built, an aid station behind the line,

846
00:38:14,512 --> 00:38:17,849
a hospital behind that, and evacuation in between,

847
00:38:17,849 --> 00:38:20,986
remains the basis of military evacuation today.

848
00:38:21,436 --> 00:38:24,982
An Army article in 2019 said his plan is still in

849
00:38:24,982 --> 00:38:28,673
effect to this day, with care organized in multiple

850
00:38:28,673 --> 00:38:29,325
echelons.

851
00:38:29,775 --> 00:38:33,535
The Military Health System put it this way in 2026:

852
00:38:33,535 --> 00:38:36,999
for the first time, evacuation was a deliberate

853
00:38:36,999 --> 00:38:40,023
military process rather than an emergency

854
00:38:40,023 --> 00:38:41,055
improvisation.

855
00:38:41,505 --> 00:38:44,741
The ambulance men with green bands are now crews in

856
00:38:44,741 --> 00:38:45,503
helicopters.

857
00:38:45,953 --> 00:38:49,040
And the lesson of Antietam, that the wounded must be

858
00:38:49,040 --> 00:38:51,830
moved fast, by trained people, with a plan made

859
00:38:51,830 --> 00:38:54,916
before the battle, is still drilled again and again,

860
00:38:54,916 --> 00:38:56,341
in exercises like these.

861
00:38:56,791 --> 00:39:00,297
In 2024, a road at Fort Sam Houston, the home of

862
00:39:00,297 --> 00:39:03,950
Army medical training, was renamed Letterman Road.

863
00:39:04,400 --> 00:39:07,872
So if you had been wounded at Second Bull Run in

864
00:39:07,872 --> 00:39:11,995
1862, you might have lain on the field for a day or more.

865
00:39:11,995 --> 00:39:15,612
Wounded inside Union lines at Gettysburg, you were

866
00:39:15,612 --> 00:39:18,433
likely in a hospital by dawn on July 4.

867
00:39:18,883 --> 00:39:20,903
The surgeons could not see germs.

868
00:39:20,903 --> 00:39:23,229
They could not save most belly wounds.

869
00:39:23,229 --> 00:39:26,229
But they learned to move you, to sort you, to put

870
00:39:26,229 --> 00:39:29,351
you to sleep, and to write it all down, so the next

871
00:39:29,351 --> 00:39:31,188
war's doctors could do better.

872
00:39:31,638 --> 00:39:33,944
So walk the road one last time.

873
00:39:33,944 --> 00:39:35,209
A soft lead ball.

874
00:39:35,209 --> 00:39:38,854
A field where, in 1861, you might have lain for a

875
00:39:38,854 --> 00:39:39,747
day or more.

876
00:39:39,747 --> 00:39:42,946
A man in a green cap band, and a stretcher.

877
00:39:43,396 --> 00:39:44,710
A barn full of hay.

878
00:39:44,710 --> 00:39:47,338
Three surgeons chosen for their skill.

879
00:39:47,338 --> 00:39:49,967
A folded cloth cone, and a deep sleep.

880
00:39:49,967 --> 00:39:52,803
A saw, used fast, because fast was safer.

881
00:39:53,253 --> 00:39:55,631
Then the fever you could not see.

882
00:39:55,631 --> 00:39:56,857
A hospital train.

883
00:39:56,857 --> 00:40:00,244
A long, bright ward with windows on both sides.

884
00:40:00,244 --> 00:40:03,632
A woman with a basin, or a poet with an orange.

885
00:40:03,632 --> 00:40:07,307
And if you lived, an empty sleeve, and a pension of

886
00:40:07,307 --> 00:40:08,100
$8 a month.

887
00:40:08,550 --> 00:40:10,034
That is how lives were kept.

888
00:40:11,034 --> 00:40:12,806
